(5660455, 820559, 46177, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your top 5 image, its lovely.</td>', 'Congratulations on your top 5 image, its lovely.', '2009-09-24 08:48:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(5660507, 820465, 60910, '<td valign="top">Congrats on your 7th place!!!  I wish it would have placed even higher but so glad it made top 10!  It''s refreshing to see something like this that is wonderful work AND gives a smile, very outside the box!!  It''s a fabulous, creative capture with pizazz and you just have to look over idiots that low rate consistently in every contest, it''s because they are idiots and they know not what they do.  <br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-09-24 09:20:36.</i></td>', 'Congrats on your 7th place!!!  I wish it would have placed even higher but so glad it made top 10!  It''s refreshing to see something like this that is wonderful work AND gives a smile, very outside the box!!  It''s a fabulous, creative capture with pizazz and you just have to look over idiots that low rate consistently in every contest, it''s because they are idiots and they know not what they do.  ', '2009-09-24 10:14:48.000000', '2009-09-24 10:20:36.000000', 0),
(5660621, 820559, 28322, '<td valign="top">Thank you all for your wonderful comments, much appreciated,</td>', 'Thank you all for your wonderful comments, much appreciated,', '2009-09-24 12:58:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(5661961, 819840, 30982, '<td valign="top">It''s a gorgeous photo - extremely well done both from a capture standpoint and a processing standpoint - quite deserving of the ribbon and amazing score, so congrats on all that.  But mostly thanks for the story!  A good photograph can often be an invitation - this most definitely fulfilled that role when coupled with this marvelous story.  Whether real or not, it''s wonderful and SO much nicer than finding N/A when opening the shot.</td>', 'It''s a gorgeous photo - extremely well done both from a capture standpoint and a processing standpoint - quite deserving of the ribbon and amazing score, so congrats on all that.  But mostly thanks for the story!  A good photograph can often be an invitation - this most definitely fulfilled that role when coupled with this marvelous story.  Whether real or not, it''s wonderful and SO much nicer than finding N/A when opening the shot.', '2009-09-25 12:00:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(5661977, 821006, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.<br/> <br/>An interesting shot, I thought this one was for deep DOF until I realized that it was not over yet.  I do actually like this shot, in your comment you said it was not HDR, but I think that the details adn depth that you brought out this could well have been a very subtle one. I love the leading line of the tree branch and the punctuations of red from the apples.<br/> <br/>This may not be the most interesting subject in the world, but I think it is simple and clean.  Your horizon is tilted a far amount.  you have amazing clouds going on, maybe if you had darkened them a little more on the edges and the ground it would have set off to be a more moodier shot and really popped that tree. Maybe a crop off the bottom would have benefited this shot as well<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. An interesting shot, I thought this one was for deep DOF until I realized that it was not over yet.  I do actually like this shot, in your comment you said it was not HDR, but I think that the details adn depth that you brought out this could well have been a very subtle one. I love the leading line of the tree branch and the punctuations of red from the apples. This may not be the most interesting subject in the world, but I think it is simple and clean.  Your horizon is tilted a far amount.  you have amazing clouds going on, maybe if you had darkened them a little more on the edges and the ground it would have set off to be a more moodier shot and really popped that tree. Maybe a crop off the bottom would have benefited this shot as well ', '2009-09-25 12:23:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(5661981, 820964, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.<br/> <br/>Interesting shot, interesting processing.  To me, this is very over processed, with HDR you are meant to see teh finer details in things. I am having a hard time with finding the details.  The shadows on the face are very harsh and are washed away with no details, the apple, to my eye actaully looks like a peach. The pants though are spot on and very well done in your processing.  They almost looked acid washed. <br/> <br/>The grain here is not helping so much, I thought you added grain till i read your notes, so maybe a <i>little</i> noise reduction would have smoothed some grain away.  But not a lot as it would take away from the grunge of this shot. <br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. Interesting shot, interesting processing.  To me, this is very over processed, with HDR you are meant to see teh finer details in things. I am having a hard time with finding the details.  The shadows on the face are very harsh and are washed away with no details, the apple, to my eye actaully looks like a peach. The pants though are spot on and very well done in your processing.  They almost looked acid washed.  The grain here is not helping so much, I thought you added grain till i read your notes, so maybe a little noise reduction would have smoothed some grain away.  But not a lot as it would take away from the grunge of this shot.  ', '2009-09-25 12:30:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(5661995, 821094, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.<br/> <br/>Nice placement for this shot and good all around comments during your voting period, even your wife thinks so!!!<br/> <br/>I think what this shot is lacking is details. It is very smooth, with HDR the finer details of a shot are meant to shine on through.  Here it is very smooth and no textures to be seen.  Back off a little on the noise adjustment or the Topaz.  Your colours are very rich , almost too rich.  Using a layer of black and white would have helped with this shot and it would have also brought out more of those details that are lacking. Your DOF could do with some  more contrast, your POV could have been a little lower, just to get teh steering wheel out of the center there. <br/> <br/>Overall, you did a great job, I am looking forward to seeing more of your work!</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. Nice placement for this shot and good all around comments during your voting period, even your wife thinks so!!! I think what this shot is lacking is details. It is very smooth, with HDR the finer details of a shot are meant to shine on through.  Here it is very smooth and no textures to be seen.  Back off a little on the noise adjustment or the Topaz.  Your colours are very rich , almost too rich.  Using a layer of black and white would have helped with this shot and it would have also brought out more of those details that are lacking. Your DOF could do with some  more contrast, your POV could have been a little lower, just to get teh steering wheel out of the center there.  Overall, you did a great job, I am looking forward to seeing more of your work!', '2009-09-25 12:38:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662004, 820734, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.<br/> <br/>Amazing clouds!!!  <br/> <br/>The clouds are the selling point in this shot, they are very well processed, clean adn very moody, which I love. Your sunset behind it is good, you could have brought out a few more colours in it though.  In post processing, grab your lasso tool, feather it to 150ish, and select the sunset.  Then use your selective colour to add more feeling to it.  This way, you will be able to only affect the sunset and nothing else in this shot.  I would do the same for the foreground but use the brightness and contrast. <br/> <br/>I think cropping some of your bottom off would have helped this with a focal point. At the moment there is none and the eye is searching for one.  <br/> <br/>This is a good shot that placed well in this challenge<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. Amazing clouds!!!   The clouds are the selling point in this shot, they are very well processed, clean adn very moody, which I love. Your sunset behind it is good, you could have brought out a few more colours in it though.  In post processing, grab your lasso tool, feather it to 150ish, and select the sunset.  Then use your selective colour to add more feeling to it.  This way, you will be able to only affect the sunset and nothing else in this shot.  I would do the same for the foreground but use the brightness and contrast.  I think cropping some of your bottom off would have helped this with a focal point. At the moment there is none and the eye is searching for one.   This is a good shot that placed well in this challenge ', '2009-09-25 12:48:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662021, 820518, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.<br/> <br/>I was reading yoru comments and then the comments during voting and a lot of people picked up on the fact that you used a fill flash for the plant.  I actually like this, I think it gives the eye a starting point in the shot which leads to your very strong leading lines that you have going on.  <br/> <br/>Your details are good, would have liked to have seen a little more detail in teh rocks though. I think for me the light on the water is off.   There seems to be no reason as to why it is there and surely your flash is not that good??  Post processing is very good, POV and DOF are excellent. <br/> <br/>Overall, very nice placement<br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. I was reading yoru comments and then the comments during voting and a lot of people picked up on the fact that you used a fill flash for the plant.  I actually like this, I think it gives the eye a starting point in the shot which leads to your very strong leading lines that you have going on.   Your details are good, would have liked to have seen a little more detail in teh rocks though. I think for me the light on the water is off.   There seems to be no reason as to why it is there and surely your flash is not that good??  Post processing is very good, POV and DOF are excellent.  Overall, very nice placement', '2009-09-25 12:56:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662034, 820486, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.<br/> <br/>Nice shot and very well controlled use of your camera in lighting this. <br/> <br/>The shot is a little green/yellow.  In post processing, tone down these or in camera use your WB to help you.  The crop at the bottom is tight and could use more space or you get up on a higher plane to take this shot.  Your leaf covered bridge is tilted and throws the whole shot off as well, not a lot can be done about this though but it is something to see next time you look through the viewfinder.<br/> <br/>Your shot is sharp and clean, you did well with this shot as nightscapes are difficult!</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. Nice shot and very well controlled use of your camera in lighting this.  The shot is a little green/yellow.  In post processing, tone down these or in camera use your WB to help you.  The crop at the bottom is tight and could use more space or you get up on a higher plane to take this shot.  Your leaf covered bridge is tilted and throws the whole shot off as well, not a lot can be done about this though but it is something to see next time you look through the viewfinder. Your shot is sharp and clean, you did well with this shot as nightscapes are difficult!', '2009-09-25 13:01:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662074, 820923, 38509, '<td valign="top">great work, congratz on the win ... it really reminds me on my "fantastic" :-) photo \0/ <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=420413" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/576/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_420413.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/576/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_420413.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/576/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_420413.jpg"/></a></td>', 'great work, congratz on the win ... it really reminds me on my "fantastic" :-) photo \0/ ', '2009-09-25 13:17:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662168, 820941, 8367, '<td valign="top">A beautiful capture! The evening colors are so very dramatic. Great job!!<br/>(They had a tent set up in the middle of the square when I visited Brussels...I was really bummed. I really had to work hard to get a shot.)</td>', 'A beautiful capture! The evening colors are so very dramatic. Great job!!(They had a tent set up in the middle of the square when I visited Brussels...I was really bummed. I really had to work hard to get a shot.)', '2009-09-25 15:01:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662199, 820794, 108891, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Lelez:</b></div><hr/><i> Nice. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thank you very much for taking time to post a comment.  I received just a few comments (more than my first post w/0 comments!).  I am appreciative that everyone took time to provide some criticism, I am grateful.  The idea of HDR is very new to me, so I accept this as part of my photographic education.  I really feel encouraged that a forum esists where folks can post and compete with each other.  I think it helps everyone.<br/> <br/>This was my first try at HDR, but I don''t think it''ll be my last.  I''m glad the opportunity came up to experience a new (to me) way of making photographs.<br/> <br/>Thanks again,<br/> <br/>Bill</td>', 'Originally posted by Lelez: Nice.   Thank you very much for taking time to post a comment.  I received just a few comments (more than my first post w/0 comments!).  I am appreciative that everyone took time to provide some criticism, I am grateful.  The idea of HDR is very new to me, so I accept this as part of my photographic education.  I really feel encouraged that a forum esists where folks can post and compete with each other.  I think it helps everyone. This was my first try at HDR, but I don''t think it''ll be my last.  I''m glad the opportunity came up to experience a new (to me) way of making photographs. Thanks again, Bill', '2009-09-25 15:10:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662212, 820794, 108891, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by hahn23:</b></div><hr/><i> Moving subjects are not easy, nor plausible, subjects for multiple HDR image blending.  Good bird capture, but the image is not natural looking. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks so much for posting a comment, I really appreciate it.  Also thanks for your positive comment on the bird capture, the original is actually not too bad.<br/> <br/>I agree with all you said, and can use your criticism to do better in the future.  The technique is unknown by me prior to this competition, but I find it fairly fascinating.<br/> <br/>With the help of folks like you who take time to comment, I can see how the image was experienced from a less biased (not me!) perspective.  That''s invaluable, <i>especially </i>when I get feedback.<br/> <br/>Thanks again,<br/> <br/>Bill</td>', 'Originally posted by hahn23: Moving subjects are not easy, nor plausible, subjects for multiple HDR image blending.  Good bird capture, but the image is not natural looking.   Thanks so much for posting a comment, I really appreciate it.  Also thanks for your positive comment on the bird capture, the original is actually not too bad. I agree with all you said, and can use your criticism to do better in the future.  The technique is unknown by me prior to this competition, but I find it fairly fascinating. With the help of folks like you who take time to comment, I can see how the image was experienced from a less biased (not me!) perspective.  That''s invaluable, especially when I get feedback. Thanks again, Bill', '2009-09-25 15:15:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662214, 820794, 108891, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Yo_Spiff:</b></div><hr/><i> Comes off to me as a bit overdone on the tonemapping. The bird does not really stand out from the busy background for me. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Actually couldn''t agree with you more.  I''m just now coming to terms with the <i>huge </i>talent base at DPC.  I find the opportunity of competing with other like minded folks, and the chance at getting unbiased criticism invaluable.<br/> <br/>Gratefully,<br/> <br/>Bill</td>', 'Originally posted by Yo_Spiff: Comes off to me as a bit overdone on the tonemapping. The bird does not really stand out from the busy background for me.   Actually couldn''t agree with you more.  I''m just now coming to terms with the huge talent base at DPC.  I find the opportunity of competing with other like minded folks, and the chance at getting unbiased criticism invaluable. Gratefully, Bill', '2009-09-25 15:18:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662221, 820794, 108891, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Mephisto:</b></div><hr/><i> what''s the use of hdr here? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thank you for your comment.  Actually, the version that is posted here is pretty distant from the original, but like you said, there''s not much dynamic range.  This is all fairly new to me, so I am using the comments from other photographers to see where I can improve.<br/> <br/>Next time I can really dig a little deeper to find a greater range, with less background clutter, and maybe even get some improved comments!<br/> <br/>I''m really grateful you took the time to look and comment, the help is invaluable.<br/> <br/>Bill</td>', 'Originally posted by Mephisto: what''s the use of hdr here?   Thank you for your comment.  Actually, the version that is posted here is pretty distant from the original, but like you said, there''s not much dynamic range.  This is all fairly new to me, so I am using the comments from other photographers to see where I can improve. Next time I can really dig a little deeper to find a greater range, with less background clutter, and maybe even get some improved comments! I''m really grateful you took the time to look and comment, the help is invaluable. Bill', '2009-09-25 15:23:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662223, 820794, 108891, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by K10DGuy:</b></div><hr/><i> I shall be rating this challenge by the following criteria:<br/> <br/>1 - Not HDR in my opinion<br/> <br/>3 - HDR is way over-done, in my opinion<br/> <br/>5 - A reasonable use of HDR, but not a very engaging photo, in my opinion<br/> <br/>7 - A reasonable use of HDR, and an engaging photo, in my opinion<br/> <br/>10 - A fantastic use of HDR, and an out of this world photo, in my opinion<br/> <br/>Your photo receives:  <br/> <br/>3 </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for taking a minute to look the photo over.  I agree, the image tends to look artificial.  Because of all the grass around the bird, the HDR tends to harshen the overall feel of the composition.  The original is actually not too bad, so maybe this particular photo was not a great choice for HDR.<br/> <br/>I value the comments I was given because they''ll help me discover how others see my work, and allow me to improve over time.<br/> <br/>Gratefully,<br/> <br/>Bill</td>', 'Originally posted by K10DGuy: I shall be rating this challenge by the following criteria: 1 - Not HDR in my opinion 3 - HDR is way over-done, in my opinion 5 - A reasonable use of HDR, but not a very engaging photo, in my opinion 7 - A reasonable use of HDR, and an engaging photo, in my opinion 10 - A fantastic use of HDR, and an out of this world photo, in my opinion Your photo receives:   3   Thanks for taking a minute to look the photo over.  I agree, the image tends to look artificial.  Because of all the grass around the bird, the HDR tends to harshen the overall feel of the composition.  The original is actually not too bad, so maybe this particular photo was not a great choice for HDR. I value the comments I was given because they''ll help me discover how others see my work, and allow me to improve over time. Gratefully, Bill', '2009-09-25 15:27:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662228, 820794, 108891, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by ColemanGariety:</b></div><hr/><i> Too busy for my taste... 5/10 </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for you honesty.  The good news is that you actully gave me a vote, and I really appreciate it.<br/> <br/>I can use the DPC forum to develop new skills that I wouldn''t even have considered outside the forum.  HDR is something I had never heard of last week, so at this time I''m <i>way </i>better off than I was prior.<br/> <br/>I value the criticism this forum can provide.  If I lived in an isolated bubble where I never interfaced with others, I might think I was Ansel Adams, and that would be very sad indeed.<br/> <br/>Thanks,<br/> <br/>Bill</td>', 'Originally posted by ColemanGariety: Too busy for my taste... 5/10   Thanks for you honesty.  The good news is that you actully gave me a vote, and I really appreciate it. I can use the DPC forum to develop new skills that I wouldn''t even have considered outside the forum.  HDR is something I had never heard of last week, so at this time I''m way better off than I was prior. I value the criticism this forum can provide.  If I lived in an isolated bubble where I never interfaced with others, I might think I was Ansel Adams, and that would be very sad indeed. Thanks, Bill', '2009-09-25 15:31:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662236, 820794, 108891, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by rkligman:</b></div><hr/><i> So overprocesed that I almost miss the subject </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for your comments.  Sometimes, I even had problems making out the bird!  I''m not even sure if the HDR were less if this particular composition would work, it''s very busy.<br/> <br/>I am grateful that you posted a comment, I find them all comments expremely useful.  This is a learning experience for me, and I look on the feedback as a tool to improve, and I think you''re spot on!<br/> <br/>Thank you,<br/> <br/>Bill</td>', 'Originally posted by rkligman: So overprocesed that I almost miss the subject   Thanks for your comments.  Sometimes, I even had problems making out the bird!  I''m not even sure if the HDR were less if this particular composition would work, it''s very busy. I am grateful that you posted a comment, I find them all comments expremely useful.  This is a learning experience for me, and I look on the feedback as a tool to improve, and I think you''re spot on! Thank you, Bill', '2009-09-25 15:36:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662566, 820932, 103142, '<td valign="top">A little late, but I realyl feel that this shot deserves a bluebird: <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=811107" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/811107.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/811107.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/811107.jpg"/></a>. Beautifully done!<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=935058" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">posthumous awards</a> <br/> <br/></td>', 'A little late, but I realyl feel that this shot deserves a bluebird: . Beautifully done! posthumous awards  ', '2009-09-25 20:33:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662572, 820610, 103142, '<td valign="top">A little late, but I realyl feel that this shot deserves a bluebird: <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=811107" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/811107.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/811107.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/811107.jpg"/></a>. Beautifully done!<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=935058" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">posthumous awards</a></td>', 'A little late, but I realyl feel that this shot deserves a bluebird: . Beautifully done! posthumous awards', '2009-09-25 20:36:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662583, 820410, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. <br/> <br/>This is the coolest shot. You have the most amazing leading lines, you are crisp, clean and clarity is tremendous.  Your HDR here shows people what it can be, not the grainy stuff we are so often confronted with.  Your POV is excellent, the half empty shows us what could be, what has been.  Your DOF is great, I really feel the height in this shot.<br/> <br/>I am not a huge fan of borders, but this suits this shot to a T.  I can imagine this on any postcard and selling like hotcakes. <br/> <br/>Well done</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club  You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.  This is the coolest shot. You have the most amazing leading lines, you are crisp, clean and clarity is tremendous.  Your HDR here shows people what it can be, not the grainy stuff we are so often confronted with.  Your POV is excellent, the half empty shows us what could be, what has been.  Your DOF is great, I really feel the height in this shot. I am not a huge fan of borders, but this suits this shot to a T.  I can imagine this on any postcard and selling like hotcakes.  Well done', '2009-09-25 20:44:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662591, 821043, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. <br/> <br/>Fun shot here, I am not sure i am seeing the HDR in this image.  Reading your notes also seem to confirm this.  HDR is usually 2 or more shots that are blended together.  BUT in saying that, the old fashioned way was using your exposure , highlights shadows, contrasts and I think you may have gone this way. As I am pretty sure that shooting from a speeding car would be hard to get 2 or more shots the same!<br/> <br/>You have amazing sharpness here, the clarity is spot on.  The cyans do seem just a tad cyan. Your POV is fair, I think if you had cropped up to the fence and had the sky as  the main focal point this would have scored a little higher.  Your DOF is excellent with everything in focus. <br/> <br/>Nice shot and I hope you had a nice holiday!<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club  You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.  Fun shot here, I am not sure i am seeing the HDR in this image.  Reading your notes also seem to confirm this.  HDR is usually 2 or more shots that are blended together.  BUT in saying that, the old fashioned way was using your exposure , highlights shadows, contrasts and I think you may have gone this way. As I am pretty sure that shooting from a speeding car would be hard to get 2 or more shots the same! You have amazing sharpness here, the clarity is spot on.  The cyans do seem just a tad cyan. Your POV is fair, I think if you had cropped up to the fence and had the sky as  the main focal point this would have scored a little higher.  Your DOF is excellent with everything in focus.  Nice shot and I hope you had a nice holiday! ', '2009-09-25 20:55:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662611, 820343, 68769, '<td valign="top">My highest score in this challenge a 10, and the only one above a 7. Well done..</td>', 'My highest score in this challenge a 10, and the only one above a 7. Well done..', '2009-09-25 21:13:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662616, 821093, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member. <br/> <br/>I would say that the only thing that is hurting you here is the over brightness and saturation of the greens.  The sky would be just perfect with if the greens where a little tuned down. <br/> <br/>Good placement on this shot and a marvelous scene, well done<br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club  You asked for a comment from a Critique Club member.  I would say that the only thing that is hurting you here is the over brightness and saturation of the greens.  The sky would be just perfect with if the greens where a little tuned down.  Good placement on this shot and a marvelous scene, well done', '2009-09-25 21:19:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662633, 820343, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club, <br/> <br/>You asked that one of your shots have a comment from one of the members of the Critique Club. <br/> <br/>This is an interesting shot. You have a very good POV going on here.  The one thing that is not going for you is that it looks like a stuffed animal. One way to avoid this is to bring more life into the eyes.  There is a great tutorial on how to make eyes pop in the Tutorial tab on DPC.  Using some of these techniques could have made this come alive versus the dead eye stare. <br/> <br/>HDR is meant to bring out all the hidden details that are not not easily captured in a single shot. But to be honest in this finished shot it is not showing too many more details than a one shot, shot. <br/> <br/>Maybe some curves, selective colours would have popped the fur as well. <br/> <br/><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-09-25 20:39:12.</i></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club,  You asked that one of your shots have a comment from one of the members of the Critique Club.  This is an interesting shot. You have a very good POV going on here.  The one thing that is not going for you is that it looks like a stuffed animal. One way to avoid this is to bring more life into the eyes.  There is a great tutorial on how to make eyes pop in the Tutorial tab on DPC.  Using some of these techniques could have made this come alive versus the dead eye stare.  HDR is meant to bring out all the hidden details that are not not easily captured in a single shot. But to be honest in this finished shot it is not showing too many more details than a one shot, shot.  Maybe some curves, selective colours would have popped the fur as well.  ', '2009-09-25 21:38:29.000000', '2009-09-25 21:39:12.000000', 0),
(5662886, 820932, 29906, '<td valign="top">This is a really nice image and should have scored higher.</td>', 'This is a really nice image and should have scored higher.', '2009-09-26 02:29:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(5662894, 820529, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I thought this was a very striking image when I was voting on the HDR challenge.  So much so, I gave it quite a high score, even though I''m not sure HDR really works that well for flowers.<br/> <br/>I think the centered composition worked very well for this image and the white against the black background was excellent.<br/> <br/>As one of the purposes of using HDR is to bring out the details in the shadows and highlights, I think it would have been nice to see a bit more detail in the centre of the flower, however that is a minor nit pick.<br/> <br/>Overall very well done image.  I suppose the main reasons it didn''t score higher is a) it''s a flower and quite often these are not appreciated on DPC b) some people did not think it was HDR.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I thought this was a very striking image when I was voting on the HDR challenge.  So much so, I gave it quite a high score, even though I''m not sure HDR really works that well for flowers. I think the centered composition worked very well for this image and the white against the black background was excellent. As one of the purposes of using HDR is to bring out the details in the shadows and highlights, I think it would have been nice to see a bit more detail in the centre of the flower, however that is a minor nit pick. Overall very well done image.  I suppose the main reasons it didn''t score higher is a) it''s a flower and quite often these are not appreciated on DPC b) some people did not think it was HDR. ', '2009-09-26 02:36:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(5663424, 819840, 46436, '<td valign="top">Magnificent story and beautiful photo..the blue is stunning</td>', 'Magnificent story and beautiful photo..the blue is stunning', '2009-09-26 17:10:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(5663761, 820858, 109478, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by dd1989:</b></div><hr/><i>The only slight issue, what''s the black spot on the light of the train on the left? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>It should be white. I guess it turned into black when I adjusted the Hue/Saturation or selective color.</td>', 'Originally posted by dd1989:The only slight issue, what''s the black spot on the light of the train on the left?   It should be white. I guess it turned into black when I adjusted the Hue/Saturation or selective color.', '2009-09-27 00:51:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(5663804, 820858, 109478, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by jmsetzler:</b></div><hr/><i> The rear of the train seems to be the focus point of the image, and it is falling dead center in the frame. I''m also struggling with the lighting, and it may be that a selective desaturation was used in this image, but I''m not sure.  The yellow cast behind the train just looks and feels strange to me when there is no other light with that tone in the scene. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for the critique!<br/> <br/>For composition, I had the same concern as you. So I tried to crop<br/>the left side. However, it looked boring without the left train and the ground, so I kept the left side finally.<br/> <br/>I changed the color by using selective color to make it looks more "industrial". And the yellow cast front the train(this is the front of the train) was red before adjusting. My wife suggested me changing it to other color, and I found it looked more mysterious in yellow. There are at least three different color lights from different direction. The lighting was indeed strange(and mysterious to me) so that I wanted to take photos in this station.^^<br/> <br/>Here is the original:<br/><a href="//www.flickr.com/photos/ldw406/3957906444/" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.flickr.com/photos/ldw406/3957906444/</a> <br/> <br/>Thanks for the critique again, you remind me that I should pay more attention on composition and lighting.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-09-27 00:55:11.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by jmsetzler: The rear of the train seems to be the focus point of the image, and it is falling dead center in the frame. I''m also struggling with the lighting, and it may be that a selective desaturation was used in this image, but I''m not sure.  The yellow cast behind the train just looks and feels strange to me when there is no other light with that tone in the scene.   Thanks for the critique! For composition, I had the same concern as you. So I tried to cropthe left side. However, it looked boring without the left train and the ground, so I kept the left side finally. I changed the color by using selective color to make it looks more "industrial". And the yellow cast front the train(this is the front of the train) was red before adjusting. My wife suggested me changing it to other color, and I found it looked more mysterious in yellow. There are at least three different color lights from different direction. The lighting was indeed strange(and mysterious to me) so that I wanted to take photos in this station.^^ Here is the original://www.flickr.com/photos/ldw406/3957906444/  Thanks for the critique again, you remind me that I should pay more attention on composition and lighting.', '2009-09-27 01:34:28.000000', '2009-09-27 01:55:11.000000', 0),
(5663811, 820858, 109478, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by gg3rd:</b></div><hr/><i> Very nice composition (no pun intended...). Intriguing that no people is shown (even the conductor). </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>In my imagimation, it would be better if there were some fog and one or two passengers walking or standing on left side. But there is some different mood that no people is shown.</td>', 'Originally posted by gg3rd: Very nice composition (no pun intended...). Intriguing that no people is shown (even the conductor).   In my imagimation, it would be better if there were some fog and one or two passengers walking or standing on left side. But there is some different mood that no people is shown.', '2009-09-27 01:38:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(5663818, 820610, 88580, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by skewsme:</b></div><hr/><i> somehow it''s a relief that it''s only flowers yelling at me this time.  actually i like this.  try a black vignette, they''re in vogue :P </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>lol...not gunna try another vignette for a while, I''d guess.<br/> <br/>eta: I meant to say how much I liked all the textures you captured in this.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-09-27 00:52:26.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by skewsme: somehow it''s a relief that it''s only flowers yelling at me this time.  actually i like this.  try a black vignette, they''re in vogue :P   lol...not gunna try another vignette for a while, I''d guess. eta: I meant to say how much I liked all the textures you captured in this.', '2009-09-27 01:51:22.000000', '2009-09-27 01:52:26.000000', 0),
